Z||000004||Jay||10-17-2001||07:11 PM||||Who's gonna donate a real F40 to pull a mold off of?  Again, Fiero is only an Italian name, not an italian car.  An F40 is made of composite materials like Kevlar and Carbon fibre.  If you've ever seen a real F40 you'd never even think of buying or building a kit.  Just my opinion <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||10-17-2001||07:16 PM||||Hello everyone.<P>GTFiero1, you're right. Most F40 replicas are wrong. I suspect its because the ones you've seen are all based from the same original kit. Archies yellow F40 is an example of that kit. Looks awesome but there are still quite a few differences from the original car. However 99% of the population just can't tell the difference.<P>To make an accurate F40 replica that is based on a Fiero donor involves a -lot- of work. You'll need to stretch the car, cut off the roof replace the doors (because the windows are completely wrong) as well as the windshield. Install new rear quarter panel windows and so-on. Doing it right is just as hard as making a Lamborghini Countach or Diablo. Almost no one goes to this length to make an F40 replica. The replica kits that are/were available were designed for ease of construction, not accuracy. It is just better and easier to build a custom chassis rather than using a Fiero donor car.<P>I know of at least 2 cases where the proper amount of work was performed. One is in New Zealand and the other is in North America.<BR>Replicas that are this accurate are very rare and aren't generally known to the public. These specific cars are actually custom built race cars that look exactly like an F40. I'm sure there is at least one more hiding out somewhere in the world.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroBUZZ||10-17-2001||07:37 PM||||I'll bet they don't have a proper interior, either. Or a V-12, yada yada yada.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jay||10-17-2001||07:43 PM||||Actually the F40 is a 3.0 litre V8 with twin turbos and an intercooler.  It has a limited interior that consists of a gauge pod, roll up windows and sometimes two seats, that's it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||GTFiero1||10-17-2001||08:37 PM||||i think they should make some replicas where its the actual length. i could be on a tube chassis. if a company puts a lot of effort to make a diablo relica look exact...why not the F-40. the one think i just dont like is the front grills on the replicas. there too small and look goofy in my opinion, they should it least make it taller/bigger more like the real car. i know they cant get molds off a real F40 but a lot of diablo and countach replica makers cant get molds off real cars either but they still look incredibly accurate (some of them anyhow) i just think a little more effort should go into making these<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Archie||10-18-2001||12:15 AM||||How about this.....<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/52.jpg"> <P>Yes, that is a Fiero. Stretched & chopped like mentioned above.<P>Cost a lot more to build, and while lots of poeple want the "REAL LOOK" not many wanted to pay a little more for that "REAL LOOK". With all the SPA lawsuits you will be lucky to find even a "BAD" copy of the F-40 anymore.<P>I wonder where you and your deep pockets were back when this car was still being built?<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jay||10-18-2001||12:39 PM||||Archie, don't get me wrong, It looks great and 99% of people will never know the difference.  I had a Ferrari dino replica at one time. Sorry to say I learned the hard way that it is never going to look or drive like the real thing. For about 20-30 thousand you could by the real thing (308,328,mondial,GT4) and always have the value in the car.  A Fiero rebody never holds it's value (just look at the cars on e-bay) and you will never get the money you put into it.<P>I'm not knocking kit cars...if you like the hobby that is great, after all most fiero kits can knock the pants off of the real thing (308's only have about 200hp).  I loved my kit.  But I personally came to the realization that the money would be better spent on a real Ferrari.  Which is what I am planning. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">No flame intended.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Archie||10-18-2001||01:33 PM||||Well I'm not "into" the Ferrari replicas very much anymore (SPA lawsuit can break you of that real quick). I do like re-bodies but not replicas.<P>And I'm not going to even start the agruements about the higher maintence costs associated with owning the real thing.<P>When I was "into" replicas I always had a rule for myself. "Never build a car you won't drive, because you may be driving it for a while."<P>The only time I ever violated that rule was when I build myself a 25th. Lambo. I hated that car..... it was built great but was a pain in the butt to get in & out of and you couldn't park it anywhere. With labor, that one cost me more than $50K to build, drove it only 1500 miles in 2 years. At the time I had a couple F-40's, a 328 & a 308 roadster (all with V-8's). Let me tell you that when you have a choice sitting in the driveway, it's a whole lot easier to jump in the 4-40 or 308/328 than the Lambo.<P>So if your into kit cars, I'd advise you to "Never build a car you won't drive, because you may be driving it for a while."<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||ds21||10-18-2001||02:11 PM||||I'm eventually going to do a kit/re-body on my Fiero but don't want to do a replicar because you'll have to admit it's not really a ***** or whatever I rather do something that's original like the Finale I really like the look! I wish there were more to choose from though <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||10-18-2001||02:12 PM||||I def agree, NO F40 replicas look right at all. Still most are great looking, but no way similar in looks to real one. Ive seen some very sharp F50's too, but still dont look even vaugly like the real ones.<P>I think only the ones made for actual public sale had the roll up windows as an option, most were pop in plexiglas. They didnt have any carpet (bare floor) and inside door handle was just a cable strap. They were only actually sold to allow them to race in competition.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Archie||10-18-2001||03:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I think only the ones made for actual public sale had the roll up windows as an option, most were pop in plexiglas. They didnt have any carpet (bare floor) and inside door handle was just a cable strap. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And if a kit car builder built an F-40 or F-50 that was as sparsely equipped as the original then he'd never be able to sell it.<P>So go figure...... they want it EXACT but won't touch it with bare floors, no A/C and ropes for inside door handles.<P>Archie||||reg||1
